#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Render the measured Office extraction follow-up, without inferring overall scores."""
import json,statistics
from pathlib import Path
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];OUT=ROOT/'reports/multiformat-quality-2026-11-12'
def read(p):return json.loads(p.read_text())
def main():
 s=read(OUT/'benchmark-final/summary.json');q=read(OUT/'quality-results.json');c=read(OUT/'change-summary.json');env=read(OUT/'benchmark-final/environment.json')
 lines=['# BulkParse extraction quality follow-up — 12 November 2026','',
 'BulkParse now preserves more spreadsheet display values, rich text, links, comments and cached chart data; EPUB CSS emphasis/visibility and custom list labels; ODF inherited/default styles and list numbering; and nested/Roman-numbered slide lists. All changes are in BulkParse’s own readers and renderer. AnyDoc and Docling remain comparison tools, not runtime dependencies. No OCR or model parsing was added.','',
 f"Audited the diagnostics for all **67 original partial outputs**. **{c['changed_outputs']} of the 81 conversion outputs changed**, all from that partial set. The final run returns **81/81**, with **65 partial and 16 complete**. Only two partial statuses resolve entirely; the other improved documents still contain unsupported features. A partial warning is not a measured percentage of missing content, and a complete status is not proof of full fidelity.",'',
 '## How close to AnyDoc?','',
 f"On **{q['checks']} selected source-grounded checks across {q['documents']} documents**, BulkParse improves from **{q['totals']['before']}/{q['checks']} to {q['totals']['after']}/{q['checks']}**; AnyDoc passes **{q['totals']['anydoc']}/{q['checks']}**. No previously passing selected check regresses. These are diagnostic development checks deliberately concentrated on known gaps, not a representative whole-document quality score. The low starting count does not mean the old parser extracted only that fraction of document content.",'',
 '| Selected behavior | Checks | BulkParse before | BulkParse after | AnyDoc |','| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |']
 for area,r in q['by_area'].items():lines.append(f"| {area} | {r['checks']} | {r['before']} | {r['after']} | {r['anydoc']} |")
 lines += ['',
 'The ten remaining checks cover EPUB book metadata/internal anchors, legacy DOC and RTF code/quote styles, inherited RTF headings, legacy PPT notes, ODT equations and ODS duration display. AnyDoc passes those ten. BulkParse now passes three checks AnyDoc misses: chartsheet cached-title extraction and two groups of spreadsheet comments. This does not establish that either tool is better overall. BulkParse emits separate chart-series tables and may include Excel’s stored comment compatibility boilerplate; AnyDoc can produce cleaner grouping. Layout, table geometry, image contents and whole-document completeness were not exhaustively judged by these checks.','',
 'The [check manifest](quality-checks.json) contains exact expectations, source-file hashes and package-part hashes. The [results](quality-results.json) include every pass/failure and unmatched pattern. Expected facts come from source structure, not merely agreement with AnyDoc. Equivalent Markdown list syntax is accepted; Markdown punctuation escapes are normalized. These cases informed development and are not an independent holdout. No LLM judge was run. AnyDoc’s published 81/100 on its private 100-document corpus cannot be compared numerically with these check counts.','',
 '## Same-machine measurements','',
 f"Machine: **{env['machine']}**, {env['platform']}. BulkParse {env['versions']['bulkparse']}, AnyDoc {env['versions']['anydoc']}, Docling {env['versions']['docling']}. Same frozen 70 upstream conversion fixtures plus 11 authored inputs as the previous report, with nine separate robustness files.",'',
 '| Engine | Returned / attempted | Median full-corpus time | Lifetime peak RSS |','| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |']
 for e,r in s['engines'].items():lines.append(f"| {e} | {r['returned']}/{r['attempted']} | {r['median_corpus_ms']:.3f} ms | {s['resources'][e]['lifetime_peak_rss_bytes']/1048576:.1f} MiB |")
 runs={e:[read(OUT/f'benchmark-final/{e}-pass-{i}.json')['documents'] for i in range(1,6)] for e in s['engines']}
 common=set.intersection(*[{r['path'] for r in v[0] if r['ok']} for v in runs.values()])
 common_times={e:statistics.median(sum(r['elapsed_ns'] for r in run if r['path'] in common)/1e6 for run in v) for e,v in runs.items()}
 lines += ['',
 'BulkParse and AnyDoc process the same 81 inputs. Docling attempts only 31 supported XLSX/XLSM/PPTX inputs and returns 25; its six existing synthetic PPTX failures remain in the timing set. Its aggregate is not a like-for-like 81-document comparison. On the **same 25 successful inputs**, medians are '+', '.join(f"{e} **{v:.3f} ms**" for e,v in common_times.items())+'.','',
 'One excluded full warm-up precedes five measured full-corpus passes with rotating engine order. Each persistent process converts documents sequentially; conversions do not overlap. Rayon/OMP/OpenBLAS environment thread settings are one. Per-document clocks include reads, parsing and Markdown export, excluding startup, IPC/output serialization, disk prediction writes and scoring. Corpus time sums those clocks. All five measured outcomes and outputs are deterministic. RSS is the macOS process-lifetime peak, including startup/warm-up and, for the Rust tools, robustness; it is not incremental per-document memory. CPU totals in the raw logs also include startup.','',
 'Compared with the historical pre-change BulkParse run (11.500 ms and 12.9 MiB), the added extraction increases time to 18.113 ms and peak memory to 13.7 MiB. Those are separate runs, not an interleaved old/new binary comparison. BulkParse is about 1.29× faster than AnyDoc in the current run. Differences in extracted content and visual processing still affect comparisons. These are tiny development fixtures on macOS, not Linux production throughput measurements.','',
 '### Per-format timings','',
 'Median document time is the median across each document’s five-run median. Failed attempts remain included. XLSM is an alias, not a new canonical parser.','',
 '| Format | Inputs | BulkParse median ms | AnyDoc median ms | Docling median ms | BulkParse partial |','| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |']
 for fmt,eng in s['per_format'].items():
  b=eng['bulkparse'];d=eng.get('docling');lines.append(f"| {fmt.upper()} | {b['attempted']} | {b['median_document_ms']:.3f} | {eng['anydoc']['median_document_ms']:.3f} | {f'{d["median_document_ms"]:.3f}' if d else '—'} | {b['partial']} |")
 lines += ['', '## General rules and remaining limitations','',
 '- EPUB reads only package-local stylesheets. Supported selectors use specificity and source order; inline declarations and `!important` participate in the cascade. Unsupported conditional/selective visibility rules cause conservative text retention plus an incomplete diagnostic. This prevents a discovered accessibility-title regression. Full CSS layout, internal exported anchors and book metadata headings remain incomplete.',
 '- ODF resolves styles by family, follows inheritance with cycle/depth limits, applies family defaults and explicit overrides, and preserves standard preformatted/quotation styles. Numbered lists retain headers, restarts and continuation. Adjacent preformatted paragraphs remain separate code blocks; rich table-cell styles, objects and equations remain gaps.',
 '- XLSX retains font emphasis and rich runs, safe links, comments, drawing descriptions/text and chart caches. Common negative/zero/literal numeric formats and elapsed hours are handled; the shared formatter also improves XLS/XLSB. Full style inheritance/conditional formatting and unusual numeric patterns remain incomplete. Cached formulas and charts are never recalculated.',
 '- PPTX preserves nested mixed lists and Roman/alphabetic labels using inherited list rules. Nondecimal labels use literal text where the shared list model cannot encode them. Charts retain cached series and source references; visuals, SmartArt, equations and spatial order remain incomplete.',
 '- HTML table rendering retains emphasis and safe links; Markdown emphasis moves surrounding whitespace outside delimiters. Legacy DOC/PPT/RTF and XLSB table/formatting gaps remain, including nonempty covered merged cells and complex legacy geometry.',
 '- No production branches use corpus filenames, document IDs, fixture text or benchmark scores. Standard format style names and syntax drive rules. Bounds, safe URL filtering, archive traversal checks and offline behavior remain in place. No third-party converter is invoked by production code.','',
 '## Validation','',
 '- **186 Rust tests pass** with all workspace features, including **26 Office tests** (nine new tests cover these changes). Minimal core: **21**; Office-only core: **47**. Workspace Clippy with warnings denied and formatting checks pass.',
 '- Existing authored semantic checks remain **11/11** for BulkParse and AnyDoc; Docling **2/2** on supported authored formats. The nine separate robustness outcomes are unchanged: BulkParse rejects eight and recovers one RTF; AnyDoc rejects seven and recovers the RTF plus a damaged-persistence PPT. No crashes in this set.',
 '- Node/Python each pass four smoke tests; WASM smoke checks pass. **19 conversion cases and three PDF-inspection cases** match across Node, Python, WASM and CLI. Extended parity passes **26 conversions and three inspections** with `node --liftoff-only`.',
 '- The previously documented default Node 24.21.0 optimizing-compiler OOM on large PDF/WASM cases is still an outstanding limitation; it was reproduced on the old implementation in the previous task. This follow-up does not claim it is fixed or that default extended parity passes.',
 '- **All 200 reference PDF Markdown outputs are byte-for-byte unchanged**, verified with the benchmarked binary. NID/TEDS/MHS inputs therefore remain unchanged; PDF scoring and timing were not rerun. This is a regression check, not a new PDF performance result.',
 '- All 67 original partial outputs have a [per-document audit](partial-audit.md), with [machine-readable diagnostics](partial-audit-after.json). The 31 changed outputs were reviewed for removed text; expected removals are CSS-hidden text and transformed numeric/list syntax. The unsupported conditional CSS removal was fixed before the final run. There is no exhaustive visual comparison of every page or proof that no untested regression exists.','',
 '## Reproduce and download','',
 'Build the source and restore the hash-matched upstream fixtures as described in the [protocol](../../benchmarks/multiformat/README.md). The evidence bundle excludes third-party inputs, dependencies, models and compiled binaries.','',
 '```sh','cargo test --workspace --all-features','cargo build --release -p bulkparse --example office_bench','python3 scripts/benchmark_office_formats.py --out reports/my-quality-run','python3 scripts/validate_office_benchmark.py reports/my-quality-run','python3 scripts/evaluate_office_quality.py --run reports/my-quality-run --out reports/my-quality-evaluation','python3 scripts/check_office_pdf_regression.py --out reports/my-quality-evaluation/pdf-regression.json','```','',
 '- [Download source and evidence ZIP](../multiformat-quality-2026-11-12.zip).',
 '- [Benchmark summary](benchmark-final/summary.json), [environment and binary hashes](benchmark-final/environment.json), [input manifest](benchmark-final/manifest.json), [source hashes](benchmark-final/source-manifest.json).',
 '- Every warm-up and measured prediction/timing is stored in `benchmark-final`; resource logs and separate robustness results are included.',
 '- [Validation record](validation/summary.json), [PDF equality evidence](pdf-regression.json), [before/after quality checks](quality-results.json), [historical baseline](../multiformat-2026-11-11/README.md).','']
 (OUT/'README.md').write_text('\n'.join(lines))
if __name__=='__main__':main()
